## Hit visualization — lifecycle, not state

A hit isn't a thing that exists on a ship. **A hit is a timestamped observation that the ship *was* in cell C at turn T.** The cell remembers; the ship moves on. This is the cognitive load-bearing wall of the whole game and the UI has to make it impossible to forget.

So a hit marker has a lifecycle:
| Age | Look |
|----|----|
| **This turn** (fresh) | Bright orange/red splash animation, bold turn-number badge, brief "ship fragment" silhouette flash that fades to just the X |
| **Recent** (last 3-5 turns) | Full-saturation X, large turn label, slight glow if part of an active contact track |
| **Tracked** (correlated to active contact) | Colored to that contact's track color, connected to siblings by a faint vector arrow |
| **Stale** (>10 turns, ship probably long gone) | Desaturated X, smaller turn label, but **never removed** — it's still evidence |
| **Closed** (ship sunk) | Recolored to the sunk-ship's "case-closed" hue, optionally collapsed into a single trail glyph |


Misses get the same treatment but in a much weaker visual register — a small grey dot, fading slightly over time, never disappearing because *the absence of a ship is also evidence*.

## The trail over time — connect-the-dots, optionally

Two timestamped hits = a vector (heading + speed). The UI should let the player **see** that vector, ideally as a toggleable aid:

* **Off**: pure raw signal. Competitive players want this.
* **On**: hits the system thinks belong to the same ship get color-grouped, a faint dashed line connects them in turn-order, and an extrapolation arrow projects forward to "predicted position this turn" with a crosshair.

The grouping logic is itself an interesting design question — does the *game* do the correlation for the player (helpful, but spoils part of the puzzle), or does the player manually lasso hits to form a "contact track" (more authentic to CIC, more work)? I'd argue: **player-drawn tracks**, with an optional "auto-suggest" that highlights candidates. The act of grouping hits into a track *is* the gameplay.

