## The yield/dodge spectrum, evaluated

Three flavors of the "smaller yields" rule, ordered by how disruptive they are to the deterministic-trajectory ideal:
| Rule | What happens | Cost to inference | My take |
|----|----|----|----|
| **Ghost ships** | Bodies overlap silently. Multi-ship cells exist but are invisible. Probe on multi-ship cell hits the top-of-stack one ship. | None | Cleanest. *Boring* given how much physical realism you crave. |
| **Yield-pause** | When two ships would occupy the same cell at tick T, the smaller defers its move by 1 turn. Heading unchanged. | Mild — attacker's speed estimates get noisy but heading inference is intact. The "cooldown" the attacker sees is the *effective* cooldown including pauses. | **My pick.** Lines stay lines; only spacing along the line wobbles. |
| **Dodge-turn** | Smaller ship deviates by 1 hex sideways for one move, then resumes original heading on a parallel track. | Heavy — trajectory is no longer a straight line, just two collinear segments with a jog. Two hits no longer collapse to a line if the jog happened between them. | Glorious chaos but the inference puzzle gets very hard. Save for v2 / "rough seas" rule. |
| **True turn-radius** | Ships have agility ratings, dodges happen continuously, paths curve. | Catastrophic to inference | Beautiful idea, wrong game. |



**Yield-pause is the sweet spot.** Here's why I like it more than ghost ships even though it adds complexity:

<ol style="--ol-counter: cr-h5BH4OBOrT; counter-reset: var(--ol-counter) 0;">
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Defender skill emerges.</b> A clever defender can place a BB on a trajectory that <i>will</i> intersect with a DD's path, knowing the DD will yield and effectively become a <i>variable-speed contact</i> from the attacker's perspective. That DD's "cooldown" as inferred from hit timestamps will be noisy — <i>and that's the defender's strategic gift</i>.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Physically intuitive.</b> Big ship has right-of-way. PT boat steps aside. Every player gets this immediately.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Lines stay lines.</b> Two hits still tell you the <i>heading</i>. They just don't tell you the <i>exact cooldown</i> anymore — and that's a sophisticated attacker problem to deal with.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Symmetry</b>: when two equal-size ships collide, <i>both</i> yield (mutual pause). Or, lower ship-id yields. Either works; I'd default to "both pause" because it's more conservative.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>

The cost is that the defender's trajectory is no longer a pure dead-reckoning vector; it's "vector with occasional stutter." But that *is* what real ships at sea do, and it gives the attacker's inference engine something genuinely hard to chew on.

## Size-based cooldown — yes, with a twist

You touched on this and it's worth elevating. **Make cooldown a function of size**, then let the defender modulate within a band:
| Ship | Size | Base cd | Defender choice | Sunk-1 cd | Sunk-2 cd |
|----|----|----|----|----|----|
| BB | 5 | 4 | 3-5 | up to 6 | up to 7 |
| CA | 4 | 3 | 2-4 | up to 5 | up to 6 |
| DD | 3 | 2 | 1-3 | up to 4 | up to 5 |
| DD | 3 | 2 | 1-3 | up to 4 | up to 5 |
| PT | 2 | 1 | 1-2 | up to 3 | up to 4 |



This is *much* more physically grounded — a battleship lumbers, a PT boat scoots. It also reframes the defender's placement decision: instead of "where on a 1-3 cooldown slider for every ship," it's "do I want this DD lively (cd 1) or steady (cd 3)?" — *contextual* to the ship.

And the second-order effect is *delicious*: a PT boat at cd 1 cycling around the board is genuinely hard to hit — but a single hit pushes it to cd 2, *making it as slow as an undamaged DD*. The relative speed ranking gets shuffled by damage. Beautiful.

Slight downside per the sim: size-based cooldowns push collision rate up from 31% → 34% because the BB sits in place longer waiting for its 4-turn cycle, accumulating "presence" that smaller ships sail through. Not a big change.

## Defender's view UI

You asked how the attacker view and defender view coexist. Here's the clean split:

**Attacker view** (the one we've been designing) is your *primary canvas*. It's where you spend 95% of your time during your turn — pointing, firing, inferring tracks.

**Defender view** is *information*, not workspace. It belongs in the **fleet panel** on your sidebar, not as a second board. Sketch:

